# Optional in CMake and named here on purpose. Without it the colour tools hand
# every ICC conversion to Ghostscript instead of doing it in process, and the
# build says so in one line of output that nobody reads. A package that quietly
# loses a feature is worse than one that fails to build.
               liblcms2-dev,
# ── What the suite needs in order to run its cases rather than skip them.
# dh_auto_test runs the whole suite, and nearly every one of these tests reports
# itself as passing when its tool is absent, so a shorter list here would turn a
# real suite into a green tick.
#
#   xvfb              the end-to-end tests drive a real window
#   ghostscript       the scan fixtures are rasterised with gs, and the
#                     compression and PDF/A cases shell out to it
#   fonts-urw-base35  gs needs a Helvetica to draw those fixtures with, and OCR
#                     over a page drawn in a fallback font proves nothing. It
#                     arrives today as a dependency of libgs10-common; it is
#                     named here because the fixtures depend on the fonts
#                     themselves, not on that chain staying as it happens to be
#   tesseract-ocr-*   with no language data ScanProcessor reports itself
#                     unavailable and the entire OCR suite skips. Both languages,
#                     because the scan tests recognise German as well as English
#   appstream         appstreamcli backs the metadata test, which data's
#                     CMakeLists registers only when it finds the program
#   python3           the four Python guards: translatable strings, duplicate
#                     shortcuts, unreachable actions, and the man page
#   libxml2-utils     xmllint, which the handbook check needs
#   docbook-xml       the DTDs locally, so validating the handbook neither
#                     reaches for the network nor fails without it
#   locales-all       every C++ test is registered twice, once under LC_ALL=C
#                     and once under de_DE.UTF-8. Asking for a locale that does
#                     not exist is not an error: the C locale quietly applies
#                     instead, so in a clean chroot half the suite would pass
#                     while testing nothing
               xvfb <!nocheck>,
               ghostscript <!nocheck>,
               fonts-urw-base35 <!nocheck>,
               tesseract-ocr-eng <!nocheck>,
               tesseract-ocr-deu <!nocheck>,
               appstream <!nocheck>,
               python3 <!nocheck>,
               libxml2-utils <!nocheck>,
               docbook-xml <!nocheck>,
               locales-all <!nocheck>
